WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.900 --> 00:00:02.335
- So, you're not gonna solve it

00:00:02.335 --> 00:00:03.970
with this incredibly costly

00:00:03.970 --> 00:00:06.206 align:start size:84% position:16%
and very, very
ineffective policy.

00:00:06.206 --> 00:00:07.573
The way you're gonna solve it

00:00:07.573 --> 00:00:09.842
is like we've solved
all other problems.

00:00:09.842 --> 00:00:11.344
Through innovation.

00:00:11.344 --> 00:00:13.312
Remember back in the 1950s,

00:00:13.312 --> 00:00:15.448 align:start size:88% position:13%
Los Angeles was a
terribly polluted place.

00:00:15.448 --> 00:00:19.052
It was mostly because of cars
and a geological, you know,

00:00:19.052 --> 00:00:20.753
it put in a basin and stuff.

00:00:20.753 --> 00:00:23.722 align:start size:97% position:3%
But fundamentally, the point
was not to tell everyone

00:00:23.722 --> 00:00:26.192
in Los Angeles, "I'm sorry,
could you walk instead?"

00:00:26.192 --> 00:00:27.660
Which would never have worked.

00:00:27.660 --> 00:00:29.228
- They do not walk in LA.

00:00:29.228 --> 00:00:31.531
- No, you're not gonna
take people's cars away.

00:00:31.531 --> 00:00:33.700
The solution was this, you know,

00:00:33.700 --> 00:00:37.070 align:start size:97% position:3%
tiny technological advance
called the catalytic converter

00:00:37.070 --> 00:00:38.538
innovated in 1978.

00:00:39.739 --> 00:00:41.274
You pluck it on
the end tailpipe,

00:00:41.274 --> 00:00:44.411
and then you can drive much
longer and pollute much less.

00:00:44.411 --> 00:00:46.212 align:start size:88% position:13%
Yeah, it costs a
couple hundred dollars,

00:00:46.212 --> 00:00:47.981
but you've actually
managed to convince

00:00:47.981 --> 00:00:50.717
almost everyone across the
world this is a good idea,

00:00:50.717 --> 00:00:52.986 align:start size:94% position:6%
and it solved a very large
portion of the problem.

00:00:52.986 --> 00:00:55.655
Likewise, the US is the country

00:00:55.655 --> 00:00:58.858 align:start size:94% position:6%
that's cut most
emissions, CO2 emissions,

00:00:58.858 --> 00:01:00.160
over the last 10 years.

00:01:00.160 --> 00:01:01.527
How?

00:01:01.527 --> 00:01:04.597 align:start size:97% position:3%
Not because of Obama or Trump,
but because of fracking.

00:01:04.597 --> 00:01:08.801 align:start size:97% position:3%
You basically, inadvertently,
made gas so much cheaper

00:01:08.801 --> 00:01:11.404 align:start size:94% position:6%
that most people switched
from coal to gas,

00:01:11.404 --> 00:01:14.440 align:start size:88% position:13%
and gas emits about half
as much CO2 as coal.

00:01:14.440 --> 00:01:16.342
So you solved a large part

00:01:16.342 --> 00:01:19.178 align:start size:84% position:16%
of the climate problem
through innovation.

00:01:19.178 --> 00:01:20.547
That's how we do it.

00:01:20.547 --> 00:01:23.216
(upbeat music)

00:01:27.386 --> 00:01:29.122 align:start size:84% position:16%
- I'm Dave Rubin,
and joining me today

00:01:29.122 --> 00:01:32.225
is the president of the
Copenhagen Consensus,

00:01:32.225 --> 00:01:35.128
an author of "Best Things First:

00:01:35.128 --> 00:01:39.499 align:start position:0%
The 12 Most Efficient Solutions
For the World's Poorest

00:01:39.499 --> 00:01:43.236 align:start size:94% position:6%
and Our Global SDG
Promises," Bjorn Lomborg.

00:01:43.236 --> 00:01:44.771 align:start size:84% position:16%
Welcome back to
"The Rubin Report."

00:01:44.771 --> 00:01:45.972
- Hey, it's great to be here.

00:01:45.972 --> 00:01:48.141 align:start size:88% position:13%
- Bjorn, I checked
right before we started.

00:01:48.141 --> 00:01:50.543
It's been over two years,

00:01:50.543 --> 00:01:54.280
which is absolutely crazy since
the last time that we spoke.

00:01:54.280 --> 00:01:55.815
I don't know how that happened,

00:01:55.815 --> 00:01:58.918 align:start size:97% position:3%
but I do know that in these
two years, the climate people,

00:01:58.918 --> 00:02:00.720
the catastrophization people

00:02:00.720 --> 00:02:03.957 align:start size:84% position:16%
have all increasingly
gone crazier.

00:02:03.957 --> 00:02:05.691
But before we dissect them,

00:02:05.691 --> 00:02:07.327
for the people that
may not know you,

00:02:07.327 --> 00:02:09.529
can you gimme a one minute bio

00:02:09.529 --> 00:02:12.532 align:start size:97% position:3%
on what gets you to doing
all the things you're doing,

00:02:12.532 --> 00:02:13.699
and writing a book like this,

00:02:13.699 --> 00:02:14.967 align:start size:84% position:16%
and then we'll
take it from there.

00:02:14.967 --> 00:02:18.470
- Sure, what I focus on
is really to say, look,

00:02:18.470 --> 00:02:21.541 align:start size:97% position:3%
we don't have unlimited funds,
we have limited resources,

00:02:21.541 --> 00:02:24.109 align:start size:88% position:13%
but there's so many
things we'd like to do.

00:02:24.109 --> 00:02:26.713 align:start size:94% position:6%
Why don't we focus on
where we can spend money,

00:02:26.713 --> 00:02:28.614
and do the most good first?

00:02:28.614 --> 00:02:31.850
That's both true for climate,
so let's do the smart stuff

00:02:31.850 --> 00:02:33.352
and not the stupid
stuff in climate,

00:02:33.352 --> 00:02:35.521
but it's also true for all
the other world's problems.

00:02:35.521 --> 00:02:37.790
Remember, we promised,
you know, to fix hunger,

00:02:37.790 --> 00:02:39.759
and poverty, and education,

00:02:39.759 --> 00:02:41.594 align:start size:84% position:16%
and all the other
problems in the world,

00:02:41.594 --> 00:02:43.863 align:start size:94% position:6%
and what this book really
does is try to say,

00:02:43.863 --> 00:02:47.567 align:start size:97% position:3%
"There's some amazingly smart
policies that we can do.

00:02:47.567 --> 00:02:49.202
Let's do those first."

00:02:49.202 --> 00:02:52.338 align:start size:97% position:3%
Whatever else you'd love to
do, let's spend money on that.

00:02:52.338 --> 00:02:54.073
But let's just get
those, you know,

00:02:54.073 --> 00:02:58.111 align:start size:97% position:3%
it's about $35 billion
globally, and make the world

00:02:58.111 --> 00:03:00.913 align:start size:81% position:19%
an impressively
much better place.

00:03:00.913 --> 00:03:03.249 align:start size:97% position:3%
- Right, so basically, I
mean, the thesis of the book,

00:03:03.249 --> 00:03:05.885 align:start size:84% position:16%
you think for about
35 billion, we can fix

00:03:05.885 --> 00:03:08.321
pretty much the major stuff.

00:03:08.321 --> 00:03:10.689 align:start size:97% position:3%
I assume by your background
there that you think

00:03:10.689 --> 00:03:13.125 align:start size:84% position:16%
some of this includes
just some plants

00:03:13.125 --> 00:03:14.360
you could probably
get at Home Depot

00:03:14.360 --> 00:03:17.096 align:start size:84% position:16%
to keep the air in
your house clean, huh?

00:03:17.096 --> 00:03:18.598
- Please give me
more plants, yeah.

00:03:18.598 --> 00:03:21.400 align:start size:84% position:16%
No, so we're not gonna
fix all problems,

00:03:21.400 --> 00:03:24.069
but we're gonna fix a
majority of problems,

00:03:24.069 --> 00:03:27.106 align:start size:84% position:16%
and what we are
essentially showing is

00:03:27.106 --> 00:03:28.875
and we worked with
more than a hundred

00:03:28.875 --> 00:03:30.710
of the world's top economists
and several Nobel Laureates

00:03:30.710 --> 00:03:34.147
to try to find out what
do we already know works.

00:03:34.147 --> 00:03:37.116
And what we find is,
for $35 billion a year.

00:03:37.116 --> 00:03:38.351
That's not nothing.

00:03:38.351 --> 00:03:39.652
I mean, I think you have it.

00:03:39.652 --> 00:03:41.453 align:start size:84% position:16%
I certainly don't have
it, but, you know-

00:03:41.453 --> 00:03:43.789
- I'm doing all right, but
that might be pushing it.

00:03:43.789 --> 00:03:46.659 align:start size:94% position:6%
- Yes, look, in the
global setting of things,

00:03:46.659 --> 00:03:48.427
that is really couch change.

00:03:48.427 --> 00:03:53.266
For $35 billion a year, we
could save 4.2 million people

00:03:53.266 --> 00:03:54.701
from dying each and every year,

00:03:54.701 --> 00:03:56.903
and we could make the
poor half of the world

00:03:56.903 --> 00:04:00.106
about $1.1 trillion better off.

00:04:00.106 --> 00:04:02.374
This is probably
the very best thing

00:04:02.374 --> 00:04:05.010
we could do for the world,
and that's why I'm saying

00:04:05.010 --> 00:04:07.680 align:start size:88% position:13%
let's at least do those
best things first.

00:04:07.680 --> 00:04:09.415
- And that's also
why you're a bit

00:04:09.415 --> 00:04:11.017
of a controversial character

00:04:11.017 --> 00:04:14.086
because you're trying to give
solutions that are different

00:04:14.086 --> 00:04:18.190 align:start size:97% position:3%
than what seem to be the
solutions given to us by say,

00:04:18.190 --> 00:04:21.160
the WEF, and the UN, and much
of our western governments,

00:04:21.160 --> 00:04:22.395
and all that.

00:04:22.395 --> 00:04:23.663
So I wanna spend most
of this conversation

00:04:23.663 --> 00:04:26.231 align:start size:88% position:13%
talking about those
solutions, but I thought

00:04:26.231 --> 00:04:29.502 align:start size:97% position:3%
why don't we get some of
the people and organizations

00:04:29.502 --> 00:04:31.236
out of the way that are
causing the problems?

00:04:31.236 --> 00:04:35.040
Like, when you see the
WEF and the 2030 Project,

00:04:35.040 --> 00:04:38.011 align:start size:97% position:3%
all of these things, the Green
New Deal here in America,

00:04:38.011 --> 00:04:40.212
all of these giant expenditures

00:04:40.212 --> 00:04:42.348 align:start size:84% position:16%
that will massively
change things,

00:04:42.348 --> 00:04:45.117
do you look at them all
as sort of these are just

00:04:45.117 --> 00:04:48.387
complete nonsensical
boondoggle pipe dreams

00:04:48.387 --> 00:04:49.555
that will never work?

00:04:49.555 --> 00:04:50.823
Like, is there anything good,

00:04:50.823 --> 00:04:53.359
is there any good nugget
in any of these things?

00:04:53.359 --> 00:04:55.894 align:start size:88% position:13%
- Well, first of all,
there's the amazing good

00:04:55.894 --> 00:04:59.365 align:start size:97% position:3%
that actually shows that a
lot of people want to do good.

00:04:59.365 --> 00:05:01.801
They really want to
be part of something

00:05:01.801 --> 00:05:03.169
that makes the world better.

00:05:03.169 --> 00:05:05.237
Now, there is a real problem

00:05:05.237 --> 00:05:07.773 align:start size:97% position:3%
with many of the things they
identify, but very often,

00:05:07.773 --> 00:05:10.343
it ends up being a
very ineffective way

00:05:10.343 --> 00:05:12.045
to try to tackle the problem.

00:05:12.045 --> 00:05:13.913
You know, take heat waves,

00:05:13.913 --> 00:05:16.516 align:start size:88% position:13%
which is in the
conversation right now.

00:05:16.516 --> 00:05:18.351
Yes, we're gonna
see more heat waves

00:05:18.351 --> 00:05:19.618
because of climate change,

00:05:19.618 --> 00:05:20.886
and climate change
is a real problem,

00:05:20.886 --> 00:05:23.155
but we need to get a
sense of proportion.

00:05:23.155 --> 00:05:26.058
First of all, remember
many, many more people

00:05:26.058 --> 00:05:29.127
die from cold and from heat,
and so we also need to remember

00:05:29.127 --> 00:05:32.064 align:start size:97% position:3%
to actually help all the
people who suffer from cold,

00:05:32.064 --> 00:05:34.900 align:start size:97% position:3%
which is mostly about getting
them cheap energy access.

00:05:34.900 --> 00:05:36.401 align:start size:84% position:16%
But also if you
wanna help the people

00:05:36.401 --> 00:05:39.539
who are suffering from
more heat, and we should,

00:05:39.539 --> 00:05:42.074
it's mostly about getting sure

00:05:42.074 --> 00:05:44.409
people access to
air conditioning,

00:05:44.409 --> 00:05:48.347 align:start position:0%
it's about having them get cheap
electricity, and of course,

00:05:48.347 --> 00:05:50.750
also make cities more livable.

00:05:50.750 --> 00:05:52.184
One of the ways
you could do that

00:05:52.184 --> 00:05:54.286 align:start size:97% position:3%
is by making greener,
making more water features,

00:05:54.286 --> 00:05:55.754
making them lighter.

00:05:55.754 --> 00:05:58.457 align:start size:94% position:6%
Los Angeles and many other
cities are, you know,

00:05:58.457 --> 00:06:00.993 align:start size:97% position:3%
experimenting with painting
the rooftops white,

00:06:00.993 --> 00:06:03.028
or painting the tarmac white,

00:06:03.028 --> 00:06:05.131 align:start size:84% position:16%
and that reflects a
lot of the energy away

00:06:05.131 --> 00:06:07.499 align:start size:88% position:13%
and actually makes these
cities much cooler.

00:06:07.499 --> 00:06:10.269 align:start size:88% position:13%
The point here is those
sorts of solutions

00:06:10.269 --> 00:06:13.572 align:start size:88% position:13%
will actually help right
now at very low cost,

00:06:13.572 --> 00:06:14.874
and help real people.

00:06:14.874 --> 00:06:16.876 align:start size:84% position:16%
Whereas much of the
argument that you have

00:06:16.876 --> 00:06:19.011
with these, you know, we
should all go net zero

00:06:19.011 --> 00:06:20.679
will have huge costs.

00:06:20.679 --> 00:06:23.416 align:start size:84% position:16%
We're talking
five, $10 trillion.

00:06:23.416 --> 00:06:25.351
This is more than what
most countries spend

00:06:25.351 --> 00:06:27.586
on education or even healthcare.

00:06:27.586 --> 00:06:31.289 align:start size:84% position:16%
And even if you did
it, it would only mean

00:06:31.289 --> 00:06:33.826 align:start size:97% position:3%
that temperatures would
rise, but not quite as much.

00:06:33.826 --> 00:06:35.261
That's no way to help people.

00:06:35.261 --> 00:06:36.829
So my point here is to say,

00:06:36.829 --> 00:06:40.266 align:start position:0%
let's focus on the smart
solution that'll actually help.

00:06:40.266 --> 00:06:42.301
- Right, and I think
one of the problems is,

00:06:42.301 --> 00:06:44.136 align:start size:84% position:16%
as I often play clips
of these people,

00:06:44.136 --> 00:06:47.006 align:start position:0%
the elites who seem to be
pushing a lot of these policies

00:06:47.006 --> 00:06:48.841 align:start size:97% position:3%
and, you know, they don't
want you to have your stove,

00:06:48.841 --> 00:06:50.442 align:start size:84% position:16%
and they're, you know,
they don't want you

00:06:50.442 --> 00:06:52.644 align:start size:97% position:3%
to have this hot water heater
or whatever it might be.

00:06:52.644 --> 00:06:53.979
We know they're on
their private jets

00:06:53.979 --> 00:06:55.281 align:start size:88% position:13%
and all of these
things, and by the way,

00:06:55.281 --> 00:06:57.249
I don't begrudge them
any of those things

00:06:57.249 --> 00:06:59.151
except for the fact that
they're trying to make sure

00:06:59.151 --> 00:07:01.019 align:start size:84% position:16%
none of us get
those luxury items.

00:07:01.019 --> 00:07:04.223 align:start size:97% position:3%
But when you say, and I quote,
"Climate change is real,"

00:07:04.223 --> 00:07:06.058
I know a certain amount of
my audience is gonna go,

00:07:06.058 --> 00:07:09.028
"Wait a minute, we've just
had it even hearing that"

00:07:09.028 --> 00:07:12.631
because they're so upset
by hearing John Kerry,

00:07:12.631 --> 00:07:15.201 align:start size:94% position:6%
and they're so upset by
watching Leonardo DiCaprio

00:07:15.201 --> 00:07:17.637
act one way on his
yacht and helicopter,

00:07:17.637 --> 00:07:18.838
and then preach another way.

00:07:18.838 --> 00:07:21.440 align:start size:88% position:13%
So when you say
climate change is real,

00:07:21.440 --> 00:07:23.175 align:start size:84% position:16%
what do you actually
mean by that?

00:07:23.175 --> 00:07:25.411
Meaning that obviously
the climate is changing,

00:07:25.411 --> 00:07:28.780 align:start size:88% position:13%
but do you mean manmade,
and how much of that

00:07:28.780 --> 00:07:31.150
is something that we
can actually affect?

00:07:31.150 --> 00:07:33.852 align:start size:81% position:19%
- So again, I work
with economists

00:07:33.852 --> 00:07:36.455
so we're looking at what
are the smart solutions.

00:07:36.455 --> 00:07:40.626
I have read, unlike I think
a lot of people, almost all.

00:07:40.626 --> 00:07:43.162
I don't advise anyone to do
that, it's very, very boring.

00:07:43.162 --> 00:07:45.197 align:start size:84% position:16%
But all of the UN
climate panel reports,

00:07:45.197 --> 00:07:47.333 align:start size:97% position:3%
and they're reasonably
sensible all the way through.

00:07:47.333 --> 00:07:48.534
They really tried to tell you

00:07:48.534 --> 00:07:51.570 align:start size:84% position:16%
what is the best
scientific evidence,

00:07:51.570 --> 00:07:55.675
and the short version is
we're pumping out more CO2

00:07:55.675 --> 00:07:57.342
mostly from fossil fuels.

00:07:57.342 --> 00:08:00.679 align:start position:0%
CO2 is one of the many
greenhouse gases that trap heat

00:08:00.679 --> 00:08:03.081
and make the world
a little bit warmer.

00:08:03.081 --> 00:08:06.852
This, all other things equal,
is going to make more problems

00:08:06.852 --> 00:08:08.187
than it's gonna make solutions

00:08:08.187 --> 00:08:10.489 align:start size:88% position:13%
simply because we built
our entire society

00:08:10.489 --> 00:08:14.593
on what the temperature was
the last hundred, 200 years.

00:08:14.593 --> 00:08:16.662
If it got colder, or
if it gets warmer,

00:08:16.662 --> 00:08:19.031
which is probably gonna be
because of global warming,

00:08:19.031 --> 00:08:20.933
that will incur a cost.

00:08:20.933 --> 00:08:23.069
This is not the end of the world

00:08:23.069 --> 00:08:24.971
as it's being, you know, sold.

00:08:24.971 --> 00:08:26.371
It is a problem.

00:08:26.371 --> 00:08:29.208
So economists and the
only climate economist

00:08:29.208 --> 00:08:31.577 align:start size:88% position:13%
to win the Nobel Prize,
William Nordhouse,

00:08:31.577 --> 00:08:34.380 align:start size:97% position:3%
estimate that by the end of
the century, if we do nothing,

00:08:34.380 --> 00:08:36.181
which is stupid and
we shouldn't do,

00:08:36.181 --> 00:08:39.151
and probably is implausible,
but even if we did nothing,

00:08:39.151 --> 00:08:42.755
the cost by the end of the
century would be equivalent

00:08:42.755 --> 00:08:45.624
to losing about 4% of our GDP.

00:08:45.624 --> 00:08:47.927
Now remember, by then
we'll be much richer.

00:08:47.927 --> 00:08:51.196
The UN estimate we'll
be about 450% richer

00:08:51.196 --> 00:08:53.165
as rich as we are today.

00:08:53.165 --> 00:08:56.868
So what this really means is
because of global warming,

00:08:56.868 --> 00:08:58.937
if we do nothing,
it will feel like

00:08:58.937 --> 00:09:02.842
we'll only be 434% as rich
by the end of the century

00:09:02.842 --> 00:09:03.809
rather than 450.

00:09:03.809 --> 00:09:05.577
That's not the end of the world.

00:09:05.577 --> 00:09:08.180
That's a problem, and
that's why I'm saying,

00:09:08.180 --> 00:09:10.048
look, if it's the
end of the world,

00:09:10.048 --> 00:09:11.751 align:start size:94% position:6%
of course you should spend
everything to fix it,

00:09:11.751 --> 00:09:12.985
but that's not what this is.

00:09:12.985 --> 00:09:15.521
It's a problem, and
we should spend money

00:09:15.521 --> 00:09:17.790 align:start size:88% position:13%
smartly on this problem,
but also remember

00:09:17.790 --> 00:09:18.991
there are lots of other problems

00:09:18.991 --> 00:09:20.726 align:start size:84% position:16%
that are much bigger
for most people.

00:09:20.726 --> 00:09:24.363
- So before we get to some of
those specific 12 solutions,

00:09:24.363 --> 00:09:26.299
what would you say to the people

00:09:26.299 --> 00:09:27.933
who are just sort of skeptical

00:09:27.933 --> 00:09:30.469 align:start size:84% position:16%
that we can do
anything about this?

00:09:30.469 --> 00:09:34.006 align:start size:94% position:6%
Meaning like, so
from an American
perspective, for example,

00:09:34.006 --> 00:09:36.676 align:start size:81% position:19%
that we're so sort
of inefficient,

00:09:36.676 --> 00:09:37.944
our politics is so broken,

00:09:37.944 --> 00:09:41.013 align:start size:84% position:16%
these people can't
build a road properly,

00:09:41.013 --> 00:09:44.583
or at least in a
cost effective way,

00:09:44.583 --> 00:09:47.353 align:start size:97% position:3%
that the idea that they could
solve any of these things

00:09:47.353 --> 00:09:49.254 align:start size:88% position:13%
is completely crazy,
and then you hear people

00:09:49.254 --> 00:09:50.656
like AOC say, you know,

00:09:50.656 --> 00:09:51.990 align:start size:84% position:16%
"We only have 12
years left on Earth."

00:09:51.990 --> 00:09:53.259 align:start size:84% position:16%
I think that was
about three years ago,

00:09:53.259 --> 00:09:54.860 align:start size:84% position:16%
so now we've got
nine, and it's like,

00:09:54.860 --> 00:09:57.363 align:start size:94% position:6%
if you think AOC can solve
any of your problems,

00:09:57.363 --> 00:09:59.598 align:start size:88% position:13%
much less solve climate
problems, you know,

00:09:59.598 --> 00:10:00.999
I got a bridge to sell you.

00:10:00.999 --> 00:10:04.169
- So I think a lot of
the intuition is right

00:10:04.169 --> 00:10:06.939
that we are essentially
embarking on something

00:10:06.939 --> 00:10:09.342
that's gonna be so
phenomenally costly

00:10:09.342 --> 00:10:11.310
that most countries are actually

00:10:11.310 --> 00:10:12.978
not going to deliver on this.

00:10:12.978 --> 00:10:15.848 align:start size:97% position:3%
I live in the EU, I
actually worry a little bit.

00:10:15.848 --> 00:10:17.449
The EU is so good
at doing stuff,

00:10:17.449 --> 00:10:19.417
they promised that we
might actually do it

00:10:19.417 --> 00:10:21.754
despite the fact that it's
gonna be phenomenally costly.

00:10:21.754 --> 00:10:24.122
But certainly, in the US
and most other places,

00:10:24.122 --> 00:10:27.192
you'll simply elect
other politicians
really start hurting.

00:10:27.192 --> 00:10:30.329 align:start size:94% position:6%
Now, that's how, you know,
how democracies work,

00:10:30.329 --> 00:10:31.863
and that's probably really good.

00:10:31.863 --> 00:10:35.835
So you're not gonna solve it
with this incredibly costly

00:10:35.835 --> 00:10:37.870 align:start size:84% position:16%
and very, very
ineffective policy.

00:10:37.870 --> 00:10:39.171
The way you're gonna solve it

00:10:39.171 --> 00:10:41.607
is like we've solved
all other problems.

00:10:41.607 --> 00:10:42.775
Through innovation.

00:10:42.775 --> 00:10:44.977
Remember back in the 1950s,

00:10:44.977 --> 00:10:47.279 align:start size:88% position:13%
Los Angeles was a
terribly polluted place.

00:10:47.279 --> 00:10:51.017
It was mostly because of cars
and a geological, you know,

00:10:51.017 --> 00:10:52.885
put in a basin and stuff.

00:10:52.885 --> 00:10:55.621 align:start size:97% position:3%
But fundamentally, the point
was not to tell everyone

00:10:55.621 --> 00:10:58.024
in Los Angeles, "I'm sorry,
could you walk instead?"

00:10:58.024 --> 00:10:59.692
Which would never
have worked, right?

00:10:59.692 --> 00:11:00.993
- They do not walk in LA.

00:11:00.993 --> 00:11:03.161
- No, you're not gonna
take people's cars away.

00:11:03.161 --> 00:11:07.166 align:start position:0%
The solution was this, you
know, tiny technological advance

00:11:07.166 --> 00:11:10.669 align:start size:97% position:3%
called the catalytic
converter innovated in 1978.

00:11:10.669 --> 00:11:12.771
You pluck it on
the end tailpipe,

00:11:12.771 --> 00:11:16.308
and then you can drive much
longer and pollute much less.

00:11:16.308 --> 00:11:17.576 align:start size:88% position:13%
Yeah, it costs a
couple hundred dollars,

00:11:17.576 --> 00:11:19.711
but you've actually
managed to convince

00:11:19.711 --> 00:11:22.381
almost everyone across the
world this is a good idea,

00:11:22.381 --> 00:11:24.650 align:start size:94% position:6%
and it solved a very large
portion of the problem.

00:11:24.650 --> 00:11:27.319
Likewise, the US is the country

00:11:27.319 --> 00:11:30.355 align:start size:94% position:6%
that's cut most
emissions, CO2 emissions,

00:11:30.355 --> 00:11:31.990
over the last 10 years.

00:11:31.990 --> 00:11:33.425
How?

00:11:33.425 --> 00:11:36.461 align:start size:97% position:3%
Not because of Obama or Trump,
but because of fracking.

00:11:36.461 --> 00:11:41.534 align:start size:97% position:3%
You basically, inadvertently,
made gas so much cheaper

00:11:42.902 --> 00:11:44.236 align:start size:94% position:6%
that most people switched
from coal to gas,

00:11:44.236 --> 00:11:46.305 align:start size:88% position:13%
and gas emits about half
as much CO2 as coal.

00:11:46.305 --> 00:11:48.340
So you solved a large part

00:11:48.340 --> 00:11:51.310 align:start size:84% position:16%
of the climate problem
through innovation.

00:11:51.310 --> 00:11:52.578
That's how we do it.

00:11:52.578 --> 00:11:54.447
Remember, if we can
innovate green energy

00:11:54.447 --> 00:11:57.483 align:start size:97% position:3%
to be cheaper than fossil
fuels, everyone will switch.

00:11:57.483 --> 00:11:59.852
Not rich, well-meaning
Americans or Europeans,

00:11:59.852 --> 00:12:03.088
but also the Chinese, the
Indians, and the Africans

00:12:03.088 --> 00:12:05.924 align:start size:97% position:3%
who are gonna be emitting most
of the CO2 in this century.

00:12:05.924 --> 00:12:10.329
So I totally get your
viewers', you know,

00:12:10.329 --> 00:12:11.563
sort of reluctance.

00:12:11.563 --> 00:12:14.700
We are trying to solve
this very ineffectively

00:12:14.700 --> 00:12:17.269 align:start size:97% position:3%
and incredibly expensively,
which probably means

00:12:17.269 --> 00:12:20.473
we won't solve it at all,
but there is a smart way,

00:12:20.473 --> 00:12:21.974
and it's called innovation.

00:12:21.974 --> 00:12:24.243
Just like most other things.
- Well, that's exactly why

00:12:24.243 --> 00:12:25.744 align:start size:84% position:16%
I wanted to have you
on because I know

00:12:25.744 --> 00:12:28.547
even for me in the last year
especially as I've watched

00:12:28.547 --> 00:12:31.283 align:start size:88% position:13%
so many of these
globalist organizations

00:12:31.283 --> 00:12:34.152 align:start size:94% position:6%
with these crazy projects,
and then the hypocrisy,

00:12:34.152 --> 00:12:37.756 align:start size:97% position:3%
and literally the gas stove
thing here in America,

00:12:37.756 --> 00:12:41.026
all of this nonsense, even
I have become more skeptical

00:12:41.026 --> 00:12:43.662 align:start size:97% position:3%
in a way that I don't
wanna be blindly skeptical.

00:12:43.662 --> 00:12:47.166
I wanna be skeptical with some
knowledge of what's going on,

00:12:47.166 --> 00:12:48.734
which is exactly
why we have you on.

00:12:48.734 --> 00:12:50.669 align:start size:88% position:13%
So let's talk about
some of those solutions

00:12:50.669 --> 00:12:53.205
because you lay out
these 12 solutions.

00:12:53.205 --> 00:12:55.708
So give me some, what's
like the easiest thing

00:12:55.708 --> 00:12:57.176
that we can start doing now

00:12:57.176 --> 00:12:59.912 align:start size:97% position:3%
probably some of which
we're doing to some degree?

00:12:59.912 --> 00:13:02.681
- So I'll tell you two.

00:13:02.681 --> 00:13:05.818
You know, it's asking, I
have 12 great solutions.

00:13:05.818 --> 00:13:07.753 align:start size:88% position:13%
You're asking me to
pick my favorite child.

00:13:07.753 --> 00:13:09.488 align:start size:84% position:16%
I'm not gonna do
that, but, you know,

00:13:09.488 --> 00:13:10.655
but I'm gonna give you two.

00:13:10.655 --> 00:13:13.292
One is to save lives.

00:13:13.292 --> 00:13:16.094
So one thing that I think
most people don't recognize

00:13:16.094 --> 00:13:19.598 align:start size:88% position:13%
is that each year,
tuberculosis still kills

00:13:19.598 --> 00:13:23.068
1.4, 1.5 million people.

00:13:23.068 --> 00:13:26.004 align:start size:88% position:13%
Last year it was bigger
again than COVID.

00:13:26.004 --> 00:13:28.407 align:start size:81% position:19%
You know, COVID
took the top place

00:13:28.407 --> 00:13:31.443
for infectious disease
killer in 2020 and '21,

00:13:31.443 --> 00:13:34.246
but otherwise, it's
been tuberculosis.

00:13:34.246 --> 00:13:36.615
We in the rich world fixed this

00:13:36.615 --> 00:13:38.050
more than half a century ago.

00:13:38.050 --> 00:13:39.585
But if you actually go back,

00:13:39.585 --> 00:13:41.920
tuberculosis was
a terrible killer.

00:13:41.920 --> 00:13:44.789
This is why, you know,
Sabine and Moulin Rouge

00:13:44.789 --> 00:13:48.727
died from tuberculosis,
and a fourth of everyone

00:13:48.727 --> 00:13:51.596
who lived in the 1800s
in Europe and the US

00:13:51.596 --> 00:13:53.198
died from tuberculosis.

00:13:53.198 --> 00:13:55.701
This was a terrible killer.

00:13:55.701 --> 00:13:57.570 align:start size:84% position:16%
It killed probably
about a billion people

00:13:57.570 --> 00:14:00.639
over the last 200 years, but
we figured out a way to do it.

00:14:00.639 --> 00:14:01.840
Now we don't have a problem,

00:14:01.840 --> 00:14:04.109 align:start size:88% position:13%
but most poor countries
is not that place,

00:14:04.109 --> 00:14:06.512
and there's a very simple way.

00:14:06.512 --> 00:14:10.048
It's unfortunately one of the
reasons why it's hard to do.

00:14:10.048 --> 00:14:12.251 align:start size:97% position:3%
You actually have to take your
medication for half a year,

00:14:12.251 --> 00:14:14.053 align:start size:84% position:16%
and most people
know that it's hard

00:14:14.053 --> 00:14:16.221 align:start size:94% position:6%
to just take your
medication for two weeks.

00:14:16.221 --> 00:14:18.823 align:start size:97% position:3%
But there's a lot of ways
to, you know, sort of gamify,

00:14:18.823 --> 00:14:20.893 align:start size:84% position:16%
get sort of
tuberculosis anonymous

00:14:20.893 --> 00:14:23.395 align:start size:97% position:3%
where everybody gets together
once a month and say,

00:14:23.395 --> 00:14:25.998 align:start size:97% position:3%
"Yes, I took my my medication
all the way through,"

00:14:25.998 --> 00:14:28.266 align:start size:94% position:6%
and, you know, you
gamify it and give people

00:14:28.266 --> 00:14:30.769 align:start size:94% position:6%
a carton of orange juice,
that kind of thing.

00:14:30.769 --> 00:14:32.371
And it may seem a little weird

00:14:32.371 --> 00:14:34.940
that you have to pay people
to do it, but if you do that,

00:14:34.940 --> 00:14:38.243
if you make sure that they
don't have tuberculosis,

00:14:38.243 --> 00:14:40.613
they don't pass it on to
another 10 to 20 people,

00:14:40.613 --> 00:14:43.448 align:start size:88% position:13%
and this is how we solve
much of the problem.

00:14:43.448 --> 00:14:44.883 align:start size:88% position:13%
You also need to screen
many more people.

00:14:44.883 --> 00:14:48.020 align:start size:97% position:3%
We've investigated and there's
lots of models that show

00:14:48.020 --> 00:14:49.287
how much is this gonna cost.

00:14:49.287 --> 00:14:52.858
It's probably gonna cost
about $6 billion a year.

00:14:52.858 --> 00:14:55.627 align:start size:84% position:16%
So again, not nothing,
but we can save

00:14:55.627 --> 00:14:57.329
almost a million
people from dying

00:14:57.329 --> 00:15:00.165
over the next half
century every year.

00:15:00.165 --> 00:15:02.334
So this is one of the
things where we say,

00:15:02.334 --> 00:15:05.504 align:start size:94% position:6%
and, you know, we're
slightly crude economists.

00:15:05.504 --> 00:15:07.740 align:start size:84% position:16%
We go in and say,
"How much will it cost

00:15:07.740 --> 00:15:09.441
and how much good will it do?"

00:15:09.441 --> 00:15:11.576
So we give you a
benefit cost ratio.

00:15:11.576 --> 00:15:13.378 align:start size:84% position:16%
We try to estimate for
every dollar spent,

00:15:13.378 --> 00:15:15.113
how much good will
you end up doing?

00:15:15.113 --> 00:15:17.916
Turns out, if you do
on tuberculosis for
every dollar spent,

00:15:17.916 --> 00:15:20.552
you'll do $46 of social good.

00:15:20.552 --> 00:15:22.754 align:start size:88% position:13%
That's just an
incredible opportunity.

00:15:22.754 --> 00:15:26.191 align:start size:84% position:16%
- So do you guys bring
these ideas to say,

00:15:26.191 --> 00:15:28.693
a guy like Bill Gates,
who's got the billions,

00:15:28.693 --> 00:15:31.796
who seemingly wants to help,
you know, by his own words,

00:15:31.796 --> 00:15:34.132 align:start size:94% position:6%
would say he wants to
help the people of Africa

00:15:34.132 --> 00:15:35.600
and help the world?

00:15:35.600 --> 00:15:38.270 align:start position:0%
Do you bring these ideas to
them, and then what do they say?

00:15:38.270 --> 00:15:41.039 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Yes, so we both
talked to Bill Gates.

00:15:41.039 --> 00:15:43.175 align:start size:97% position:3%
Actually, the Gates Foundation
paid for this project.

00:15:43.175 --> 00:15:45.377
But we talked to Bill Gates,

00:15:45.377 --> 00:15:47.346
I wrote an op-ed
together with him.

00:15:47.346 --> 00:15:50.682
We talked to USAID and
other organizations,

00:15:50.682 --> 00:15:52.283
but crucially we
also talk to a lot

00:15:52.283 --> 00:15:54.987 align:start size:94% position:6%
of poor country
governments who obviously

00:15:54.987 --> 00:15:56.655
should also pony up
some of this money

00:15:56.655 --> 00:15:58.556
because it's their own
citizens who are dying,

00:15:58.556 --> 00:16:01.093
and much of this
is they're dying

00:16:01.093 --> 00:16:03.028 align:start size:81% position:19%
because they're
not the, you know,

00:16:03.028 --> 00:16:05.564 align:start size:97% position:3%
they're not the rich people
in the poor part of the world,

00:16:05.564 --> 00:16:07.499
if you're rich in the
poor part of the world,

00:16:07.499 --> 00:16:09.234 align:start size:97% position:3%
you don't get tuberculosis,
and even if you do,

00:16:09.234 --> 00:16:10.502
you don't die from it.

00:16:10.502 --> 00:16:12.204
But, you know, the
slum populations,

00:16:12.204 --> 00:16:14.372 align:start size:97% position:3%
it's the migrant
populations, it's the minors,

00:16:14.372 --> 00:16:16.975 align:start size:97% position:3%
it's the prison
populations, but the problem

00:16:16.975 --> 00:16:20.012
is this percolate all the
way through societies.

00:16:20.012 --> 00:16:22.381
So we try to take all
of these arguments,

00:16:22.381 --> 00:16:25.717 align:start size:94% position:6%
and make people aware that
here's a very cheap way

00:16:25.717 --> 00:16:28.253
to make the world
much, much better.

00:16:28.253 --> 00:16:30.956 align:start size:97% position:3%
And yes, they say, "Oh,
that's really interesting."

00:16:30.956 --> 00:16:34.092
Now they're not gonna say, oh
sure, so here's $6 billion.

00:16:34.092 --> 00:16:35.660
Make it go away, Bjorn.

00:16:35.660 --> 00:16:36.929 align:start size:84% position:16%
First of all, that's
not what I do.

00:16:36.929 --> 00:16:38.163
I'm an academic.

00:16:38.163 --> 00:16:40.165
I wouldn't know what
to do with 6 billion.

00:16:40.165 --> 00:16:41.566
And there's a lot
of organizations

00:16:41.566 --> 00:16:43.068 align:start size:84% position:16%
that are actually
really good at this.

00:16:43.068 --> 00:16:47.672
So you should give it to your
national healthcare systems,

00:16:47.672 --> 00:16:49.674
and the, you know,
Stop TB Campaign,

00:16:49.674 --> 00:16:51.843
and many others in
those countries.

00:16:51.843 --> 00:16:53.545
But the point is, we're
trying to make it easier

00:16:53.545 --> 00:16:55.780
for politicians, and
for philanthropists,

00:16:55.780 --> 00:16:59.184 align:start size:97% position:3%
and for development
organizations to spend right,

00:16:59.184 --> 00:17:01.419
to spend it where
it really matters.

00:17:01.419 --> 00:17:03.188
- So all right, before
we get to that second one

00:17:03.188 --> 00:17:04.656
that you like outta these 12,

00:17:04.656 --> 00:17:06.158
and then we'll get to
as many as possible,

00:17:06.158 --> 00:17:08.827
how do you make sure that
the mechanisms are in place

00:17:08.827 --> 00:17:10.662
that these things
just don't become

00:17:10.662 --> 00:17:12.931 align:start size:94% position:6%
what seemingly most
government things become,

00:17:12.931 --> 00:17:15.834
which are giant, you know,
government waste projects?

00:17:15.834 --> 00:17:17.502
We give money to everybody,

00:17:17.502 --> 00:17:19.004 align:start size:84% position:16%
we never get
receipts on anything.

00:17:19.004 --> 00:17:21.073
I think that that's another
thing that a lot of people

00:17:21.073 --> 00:17:22.674
are worried about these days.

00:17:22.674 --> 00:17:26.144 align:start size:97% position:3%
Like, even if you can convince
them of some of the risks

00:17:26.144 --> 00:17:28.080
and why we can do
some good things,

00:17:28.080 --> 00:17:29.514
they're just like, ah, you know,

00:17:29.514 --> 00:17:31.649 align:start size:97% position:3%
we're just gonna pour
money, and maybe it'll work,

00:17:31.649 --> 00:17:33.552
maybe it won't, but
we just won't know.

00:17:33.552 --> 00:17:36.922
- And that's a very
correct concern,

00:17:36.922 --> 00:17:40.058
and a lot of the things
that we spend money on

00:17:40.058 --> 00:17:41.860
go to things that
make you feel good,

00:17:41.860 --> 00:17:46.297
things that look good on TV,
but have very little effect.

00:17:46.297 --> 00:17:48.967
But we are actually using
the best models of things

00:17:48.967 --> 00:17:51.903
that have already been
done where we showcase,

00:17:51.903 --> 00:17:53.605 align:start size:81% position:19%
look, if you spend
the money here,

00:17:53.605 --> 00:17:55.207
even though some of it
is gonna go to waste,

00:17:55.207 --> 00:17:57.476
you know, that's probably true

00:17:57.476 --> 00:17:58.943
almost everywhere in the world,

00:17:58.943 --> 00:18:00.846 align:start size:97% position:3%
and some of it is just gonna
be spent in competently.

00:18:00.846 --> 00:18:02.614
That's just the way
the world works.

00:18:02.614 --> 00:18:05.617 align:start size:84% position:16%
We've taken that
into our calculation,

00:18:05.617 --> 00:18:07.952
so we're not assuming
that this is gonna be

00:18:07.952 --> 00:18:10.856
heroically spent in the
very best possible way.

00:18:10.856 --> 00:18:14.058 align:start size:88% position:13%
It's gonna be spent
reasonably competently.

00:18:14.058 --> 00:18:16.328
Like, you know, you
would actually hope

00:18:16.328 --> 00:18:17.962
you can reasonably do this.

00:18:17.962 --> 00:18:20.198 align:start size:94% position:6%
And let me share this
because it's much clearer

00:18:20.198 --> 00:18:23.468
with the other proposal
I was gonna talk about,

00:18:23.468 --> 00:18:24.903
namely education.

00:18:24.903 --> 00:18:27.840 align:start size:97% position:3%
Education is something
everyone agrees both sucks,

00:18:27.840 --> 00:18:29.608
and we should do much
more about, right?

00:18:29.608 --> 00:18:33.044
But it especially sucks in
the poor part of the world.

00:18:33.044 --> 00:18:36.815 align:start size:97% position:3%
So there's almost half a
billion kids in primary school

00:18:36.815 --> 00:18:38.850
in the poor part of the world.

00:18:38.850 --> 00:18:40.519
We got almost all
kids in school.

00:18:40.519 --> 00:18:43.554
That's great, but they're
learning virtually nothing.

00:18:43.554 --> 00:18:46.391 align:start size:94% position:6%
We say that they
technically learn to read,

00:18:46.391 --> 00:18:48.193 align:start size:81% position:19%
but if you ask
'em this question,

00:18:48.193 --> 00:18:49.862
so you ask 'em to
read this question,

00:18:49.862 --> 00:18:51.463
this is a 10 year old, right?

00:18:51.463 --> 00:18:56.367
VJ has a red hat, blue
shirt, and yellow shoes.

00:18:56.367 --> 00:18:57.903
What color is the hat?

00:18:58.804 --> 00:19:00.138
It's red, right?

00:19:00.138 --> 00:19:01.606 align:start size:84% position:16%
But 80%-
- I got it, I got it.

00:19:01.606 --> 00:19:02.874
- Thank you. Thank you.

00:19:02.874 --> 00:19:03.775
So you're there.

00:19:04.842 --> 00:19:07.879
80% of kids cannot
answer this question,

00:19:07.879 --> 00:19:09.081 align:start size:84% position:16%
and it's not because
they're dumb.

00:19:09.081 --> 00:19:10.681 align:start size:84% position:16%
It's because they
can't actually string

00:19:10.681 --> 00:19:13.551
all these words together
into a meaningful sentence,

00:19:13.551 --> 00:19:15.754 align:start size:88% position:13%
and of course that
means we've technically

00:19:15.754 --> 00:19:17.956
taught them to read, but
they can't actually use it

00:19:17.956 --> 00:19:20.792 align:start size:94% position:6%
to become more productive,
and become richer,

00:19:20.792 --> 00:19:23.428
and more resilient and do
all the wonderful things.

00:19:24.763 --> 00:19:28.733 align:start position:0%
It is that they haven't actually
learned most of the stuff.

00:19:28.733 --> 00:19:33.137
So what we find is you should
spend more money on education,

00:19:33.137 --> 00:19:34.906
but this is exactly
where you would say,

00:19:34.906 --> 00:19:37.676 align:start size:97% position:3%
"Oh, but there's a lot of
ways you can spend it badly,"

00:19:37.676 --> 00:19:39.210
and that's absolutely true.

00:19:39.210 --> 00:19:42.313
So one example that I give
in the book is Indonesia,

00:19:42.313 --> 00:19:43.882 align:start size:84% position:16%
and, you know,
bless their hearts,

00:19:43.882 --> 00:19:45.349
they really wanna do good.

00:19:45.349 --> 00:19:48.786 align:start size:97% position:3%
They basically said, "We
care so much about education

00:19:48.786 --> 00:19:51.956
that we're gonna double
spending on education."

00:19:51.956 --> 00:19:55.026
So they ended up hiring more
than a million new teachers,

00:19:55.026 --> 00:19:58.296
they doubled the pay for
each of these teachers,

00:19:58.296 --> 00:20:00.298
and because of the
way they did it.

00:20:00.298 --> 00:20:02.234
So they did it in different
regions at different times,

00:20:02.234 --> 00:20:05.204
there's actually big study
that could sort of look this

00:20:05.204 --> 00:20:07.839 align:start size:88% position:13%
as a pseudo-random trial
where you could see,

00:20:07.839 --> 00:20:10.541
well, how much good does this
actually do for the schools?

00:20:10.541 --> 00:20:12.177
And it turns out,
this famous paper

00:20:12.177 --> 00:20:15.180 align:start size:97% position:3%
is called Double for Nothing,
and it basically shows

00:20:15.180 --> 00:20:17.348 align:start size:84% position:16%
yes, you spent
twice as much money,

00:20:17.348 --> 00:20:20.718
and there was no impact
whatsoever on teaching.

00:20:20.718 --> 00:20:23.921 align:start size:97% position:3%
It's very, very easy to spend
money badly on education,

00:20:23.921 --> 00:20:25.423
and this is true
everywhere else.

00:20:25.423 --> 00:20:28.760
But what we identify
are three great ways

00:20:28.760 --> 00:20:29.995
that are very, very well tested.

00:20:29.995 --> 00:20:31.463 align:start size:84% position:16%
I'm just gonna tell
you one of 'em.

00:20:31.463 --> 00:20:33.865
So the problem in most schools,

00:20:33.865 --> 00:20:35.467
but especially in
poor countries,

00:20:35.467 --> 00:20:37.836 align:start size:84% position:16%
is you have 50 kids
in this one, you know,

00:20:37.836 --> 00:20:38.937
in this fourth grade.

00:20:38.937 --> 00:20:40.239
They're all 12 years old,

00:20:40.239 --> 00:20:43.141
but they have wildly
different abilities.

00:20:43.141 --> 00:20:45.544
You know, some of 'em are
far ahead of the teacher,

00:20:45.544 --> 00:20:48.146
some of 'em have virtually
no clue what's going on,

00:20:48.146 --> 00:20:50.015
and what's a teacher gonna do?

00:20:50.015 --> 00:20:52.251
He's gonna try to teach
somewhere in the middle,

00:20:52.251 --> 00:20:54.786 align:start size:88% position:13%
and, you know, some
kids are gonna be bored,

00:20:54.786 --> 00:20:56.622 align:start size:84% position:16%
lots of kids are
gonna be totally lost.

00:20:56.622 --> 00:20:58.357 align:start size:84% position:16%
He should teach each
one of these kids

00:20:58.357 --> 00:21:01.392
at his or her own level, but
of course you can't do that

00:21:01.392 --> 00:21:02.894
if you have 50 kids.

00:21:02.894 --> 00:21:05.864 align:start size:97% position:3%
But if you put each of these
kids in front of a tablet

00:21:05.864 --> 00:21:08.600 align:start size:94% position:6%
with educational software,
just one hour a day,

00:21:08.600 --> 00:21:11.736 align:start size:84% position:16%
this tablet, actually
the software,

00:21:11.736 --> 00:21:14.939
will very quickly figure out
where exactly are you on this?

00:21:14.939 --> 00:21:17.876
You know, are you really
ahead of this curve or not?

00:21:17.876 --> 00:21:20.412
And teach you at
your exact level.

00:21:20.412 --> 00:21:22.281
So the rest of the
school will still be

00:21:22.281 --> 00:21:23.915 align:start size:84% position:16%
this boring old school
where most people,

00:21:23.915 --> 00:21:28.086 align:start size:94% position:6%
kids will be lost or
bored, but one hour a day,

00:21:28.086 --> 00:21:29.588
they'll actually learn.

00:21:29.588 --> 00:21:31.757
And what it turns out, and
there's lots of evidence

00:21:31.757 --> 00:21:35.660
that for $21 a year per student,

00:21:35.660 --> 00:21:36.995
they're not gonna
get the tablet,

00:21:36.995 --> 00:21:38.629 align:start size:88% position:13%
it's gonna be shared
with a lot of other kids

00:21:38.629 --> 00:21:40.398
and all that stuff, and
you also need, you know,

00:21:40.398 --> 00:21:42.968
solar panels in places where
they don't have electricity,

00:21:42.968 --> 00:21:45.971
you need a locker for the tablet

00:21:45.971 --> 00:21:47.706 align:start size:84% position:16%
so they don't get
stolen and all this,

00:21:47.706 --> 00:21:50.242
but it actually turns
out that for every year

00:21:50.242 --> 00:21:52.344
they go to school,
they will now learn

00:21:52.344 --> 00:21:53.679
three years of schooling.

00:21:53.679 --> 00:21:55.914 align:start size:84% position:16%
Now it's still
pretty bad schooling,

00:21:55.914 --> 00:21:57.382
so it's not like phenomenal.

00:21:57.382 --> 00:21:58.750 align:start size:94% position:6%
They're not gonna be
Einsteins just from this,

00:21:58.750 --> 00:22:01.553
but this is a way that
we can actually make

00:22:01.553 --> 00:22:04.422 align:start size:84% position:16%
almost half a
billion kids smarter,

00:22:04.422 --> 00:22:06.390
and what that means
is that they will

00:22:06.390 --> 00:22:09.394 align:start position:0%
when they become adults, go
out and be much more productive

00:22:09.394 --> 00:22:12.097
and actually make their
nations much richer,

00:22:12.097 --> 00:22:13.965
and of course that
will solve a lot

00:22:13.965 --> 00:22:15.533
of the other problems they have.

00:22:15.533 --> 00:22:19.204
So we estimate this will cost
globally about $10 billion.

00:22:19.204 --> 00:22:21.873
So again, not nothing,
but it will actually

00:22:21.873 --> 00:22:26.645
generate benefits worth $600
billion each and every year.

00:22:26.645 --> 00:22:28.513 align:start size:84% position:16%
This is just amazing
and astounding.

00:22:28.513 --> 00:22:30.014
So instead of spending it badly,

00:22:30.014 --> 00:22:33.017
we should spend it on this
really, really effective way.

00:22:33.017 --> 00:22:36.054 align:start position:0%
- You know, it's funny, I'm
reminded of a "Simpsons" episode

00:22:36.054 --> 00:22:37.622
from probably around 1993

00:22:37.622 --> 00:22:39.257 align:start size:84% position:16%
where Bart is
struggling in school.

00:22:39.257 --> 00:22:40.792
So they put him in
the slower class,

00:22:40.792 --> 00:22:42.360
and then he looks at the teacher

00:22:42.360 --> 00:22:44.529
in the slower class and goes,
"Let me get this straight.

00:22:44.529 --> 00:22:46.030
I'm struggling in
the other class,

00:22:46.030 --> 00:22:48.500
so now you've put me
in a slower class.

00:22:48.500 --> 00:22:49.901
How does this make any
sense to you people?"

00:22:49.901 --> 00:22:51.436
So you're trying to solve that.

00:22:51.436 --> 00:22:54.373 align:start size:97% position:3%
Okay, so it seems like between
education and tuberculosis,

00:22:54.373 --> 00:22:57.175 align:start size:94% position:6%
if my math is correct,
we're at about 16 billion,

00:22:57.175 --> 00:23:00.178
which is about half
of what you need

00:23:00.178 --> 00:23:01.413
to fix some of the problems.

00:23:01.413 --> 00:23:03.982 align:start size:88% position:13%
So we've got about eight
or nine minutes left.

00:23:03.982 --> 00:23:05.717
Let's plow through
a couple others,

00:23:05.717 --> 00:23:07.519
let's get to that 32.

00:23:07.519 --> 00:23:11.890
- Tell you one other, so
one that I think amazed me

00:23:11.890 --> 00:23:15.326
is the fact that maternity,
and especially pregnancy,

00:23:15.326 --> 00:23:17.996
and especially birth,
is terribly dangerous.

00:23:17.996 --> 00:23:19.897 align:start size:81% position:19%
So it turns out
about 300,000 moms

00:23:19.897 --> 00:23:24.502 align:start position:0%
die each year around pregnancy,
and about 2.3 million kids

00:23:24.502 --> 00:23:27.539
die in the first 28 days
in their life on Earth.

00:23:27.539 --> 00:23:28.973
- [Dave] Wow.

00:23:28.973 --> 00:23:30.875 align:start size:97% position:3%
- And this is not rocket
science how we deal with this.

00:23:30.875 --> 00:23:35.113
This is simply about getting
the women into facilities,

00:23:35.113 --> 00:23:38.216
so about two thirds of them
give birth in facilities.

00:23:38.216 --> 00:23:41.119
We need to get like 90%
of 'em into facilities,

00:23:41.119 --> 00:23:42.888 align:start size:84% position:16%
and these facilities
need to have

00:23:42.888 --> 00:23:45.389
basic emergency obstetric care.

00:23:45.389 --> 00:23:47.792 align:start size:94% position:6%
This is something the
World Health Organization,

00:23:47.792 --> 00:23:49.594
lots of institutions have shown.

00:23:49.594 --> 00:23:51.129
What will that take?

00:23:51.129 --> 00:23:53.064
You know, it's about having,
I don't know, disinfectant.

00:23:53.064 --> 00:23:55.567
You would imagine that
would be sort of obvious,

00:23:55.567 --> 00:23:57.969
but actually a lot of 'em
don't have clean water

00:23:57.969 --> 00:23:59.971
so that you can wash
down the surfaces,

00:23:59.971 --> 00:24:03.140
but it's also something
so simple as it turns out

00:24:03.140 --> 00:24:07.078
that about 700,000
kids each year die

00:24:07.078 --> 00:24:08.646
because they never
start to breath.

00:24:08.646 --> 00:24:12.184 align:start size:94% position:6%
So they come out, you
know, they're given birth,

00:24:12.184 --> 00:24:13.752
but then they don't
start breathing.

00:24:13.752 --> 00:24:16.654 align:start size:84% position:16%
Now this also happened
in rich countries,

00:24:16.654 --> 00:24:19.658
so about 80% of all kids that
come out of the mom, you know,

00:24:19.658 --> 00:24:21.092
just start breathing right away.

00:24:21.092 --> 00:24:25.096
15% need this slap in
the back to get going,

00:24:25.096 --> 00:24:28.900
but the last 5% don't, and,
you know, in the rich world,

00:24:28.900 --> 00:24:31.636
of course, we just simply
put a mask over their mouth

00:24:31.636 --> 00:24:34.406 align:start size:97% position:3%
and put positive air pressure,
put it into their lungs,

00:24:34.406 --> 00:24:35.607
and they go (inhales deeply).

00:24:35.607 --> 00:24:37.608
And then they go, and
then we save them.

00:24:37.608 --> 00:24:40.445
But we don't have
that in about half

00:24:40.445 --> 00:24:42.647 align:start size:84% position:16%
of all the poor
parts of the world.

00:24:42.647 --> 00:24:46.985
This hand pump costs what, $75?

00:24:46.985 --> 00:24:49.921 align:start size:84% position:16%
And it could probably
save 25 lives

00:24:49.921 --> 00:24:52.123
over its three year life period.

00:24:52.123 --> 00:24:53.758
That's the kind of thing.

00:24:53.758 --> 00:24:54.993 align:start size:81% position:19%
So there's a whole
list of things.

00:24:54.993 --> 00:24:58.063 align:start size:84% position:16%
This will cost about
$5 billion again,

00:24:58.063 --> 00:25:02.733
but it will save 166,000 moms

00:25:02.733 --> 00:25:05.504
from dying each and every
year, and it will save

00:25:05.504 --> 00:25:08.206 align:start size:84% position:16%
1.2 million kids
each and every year.

00:25:08.206 --> 00:25:09.841
How are we not doing that?

00:25:09.841 --> 00:25:12.243
So, you know, that's
another amazing thing

00:25:12.243 --> 00:25:16.280 align:start position:0%
actually every dollar spent
will deliver $87 worth of good.

00:25:16.280 --> 00:25:17.949
So there's malaria.

00:25:17.949 --> 00:25:20.885
- Yeah, you've got me to, I
see about 21 million so far.

00:25:20.885 --> 00:25:22.087
We're getting there.

00:25:22.087 --> 00:25:23.955 align:start size:84% position:16%
- So, and of course
I'm telling you

00:25:23.955 --> 00:25:26.324 align:start size:84% position:16%
the sort of more
expensive stuff first

00:25:26.324 --> 00:25:28.326
because they're the
really big things.

00:25:28.326 --> 00:25:29.761
Malaria is actually

00:25:29.761 --> 00:25:32.197 align:start size:94% position:6%
it used to be a huge
problem around the world.

00:25:32.197 --> 00:25:33.965 align:start size:84% position:16%
It's no longer,
it's just in Africa.

00:25:33.965 --> 00:25:35.166
There's a lot of
reasons for that,

00:25:35.166 --> 00:25:37.068
but we know how to fix it.

00:25:37.068 --> 00:25:39.704
It's simply giving
out more of these

00:25:40.872 --> 00:25:42.640
insecticide treated bed nets

00:25:42.640 --> 00:25:44.776 align:start size:84% position:16%
so people will sleep
under these nets.

00:25:44.776 --> 00:25:46.243
They both physically make sure

00:25:46.243 --> 00:25:48.646 align:start size:94% position:6%
that the mosquitoes can't
come in and bite you,

00:25:48.646 --> 00:25:51.048
and it also kills 'em off
because as insecticide.

00:25:51.048 --> 00:25:53.818
It is very effective,
very simple treatment.

00:25:53.818 --> 00:25:56.721 align:start size:84% position:16%
It'll cost about
$1.1 billion a year,

00:25:56.721 --> 00:26:00.959 align:start size:97% position:3%
and it will save about 300,000
people each and every year.

00:26:00.959 --> 00:26:02.994
Again, a fantastic investment.

00:26:02.994 --> 00:26:04.228
One other thing that I think

00:26:04.228 --> 00:26:06.130 align:start size:84% position:16%
you'll find
incredibly interesting

00:26:06.130 --> 00:26:08.366 align:start size:88% position:13%
is agricultural research
and development.

00:26:08.366 --> 00:26:10.234 align:start size:84% position:16%
So, you know, we
worry about the fact

00:26:10.234 --> 00:26:12.003
that people don't
have enough food.

00:26:12.003 --> 00:26:14.806
We also have part, I'm not
gonna tell you about that,

00:26:14.806 --> 00:26:17.242 align:start size:97% position:3%
we also talk about how we
can get better food to kids,

00:26:17.242 --> 00:26:18.476
but it turns out a lot of that

00:26:18.476 --> 00:26:22.113
is very corruption prone, right?

00:26:22.113 --> 00:26:24.549
If you spend a lot
of money on food,

00:26:24.549 --> 00:26:26.717 align:start size:97% position:3%
a lot of people will see
that, for instance, in India,

00:26:26.717 --> 00:26:29.253
a lot of merchants will
sell you really bad food

00:26:29.253 --> 00:26:31.356
and then you try to
distribute for free

00:26:31.356 --> 00:26:32.523
and nobody really wants it

00:26:32.523 --> 00:26:34.593 align:start size:88% position:13%
because it tastes
terrible kind of thing.

00:26:34.593 --> 00:26:37.962
So the best way to
deal with this is to,

00:26:37.962 --> 00:26:41.132 align:start position:0%
just like we talked about
before with climate, innovation.

00:26:41.132 --> 00:26:43.234
We've had a green revolution

00:26:43.234 --> 00:26:45.937 align:start size:94% position:6%
back in the '60s and '70s
which basically meant

00:26:45.937 --> 00:26:48.640 align:start size:88% position:13%
that we could produce
maybe twice as much food

00:26:48.640 --> 00:26:52.143 align:start size:97% position:3%
on the same acre of land,
which is a great innovation.

00:26:52.143 --> 00:26:55.013
You just simply make the
seed much more productive.

00:26:55.013 --> 00:26:56.815
We need that for all the stuff

00:26:56.815 --> 00:26:58.583
they grow in the poor
part of the world.

00:26:58.583 --> 00:27:01.085 align:start size:97% position:3%
So that's sorghum, and
cassava, and all these things

00:27:01.085 --> 00:27:02.687
that you probably
haven't heard of,

00:27:02.687 --> 00:27:05.290
but we need the same
green revolution there.

00:27:05.290 --> 00:27:08.593
We estimate this would cost
about $5.5 billion a year,

00:27:08.593 --> 00:27:11.396 align:start size:84% position:16%
but it would generate
much more food,

00:27:11.396 --> 00:27:14.865
but that would both means
the price would be lower

00:27:14.865 --> 00:27:16.500 align:start size:84% position:16%
for people who
live in the cities,

00:27:16.500 --> 00:27:18.903 align:start position:0%
but it would mean that the
farmers could produce much more

00:27:18.903 --> 00:27:20.938 align:start size:84% position:16%
so the farmers would
also get richer.

00:27:20.938 --> 00:27:23.541
Everyone would win,
and we'd avoid about

00:27:23.541 --> 00:27:26.945 align:start size:97% position:3%
a hundred million people
starving each and every year.

00:27:26.945 --> 00:27:30.048
- Going on that for a moment,
how much tension do you see

00:27:30.048 --> 00:27:32.951
between big cities and
rural areas these days?

00:27:32.951 --> 00:27:34.552
You know, I was in Cali before

00:27:34.552 --> 00:27:36.821
where there was always
this incredible tension

00:27:36.821 --> 00:27:38.789 align:start size:84% position:16%
between say Los
Angeles where I was.

00:27:38.789 --> 00:27:40.525
Obviously you have, you
know, 8 million people,

00:27:40.525 --> 00:27:42.961
something like that in
a relatively small area.

00:27:42.961 --> 00:27:44.462 align:start size:88% position:13%
Then you have the whole
north of the state,

00:27:44.462 --> 00:27:45.764
which is all the agricultural,

00:27:45.764 --> 00:27:47.432 align:start size:88% position:13%
and they were always
fighting for more water.

00:27:47.432 --> 00:27:49.033
The people of LA
wanted the water.

00:27:49.033 --> 00:27:52.403 align:start position:0%
Just the general like day-to-day
living of who gets what.

00:27:52.403 --> 00:27:56.841
- Oh, and look, cities get
a lot more money everywhere

00:27:56.841 --> 00:27:58.910
because that's where,
you know, the power is,

00:27:58.910 --> 00:28:00.511
that's where the
politicians live,

00:28:00.511 --> 00:28:03.147 align:start size:97% position:3%
that's, you know, who runs
the media and everything else.

00:28:03.147 --> 00:28:06.417
And so in that sense,
what we try to pick up

00:28:06.417 --> 00:28:09.954 align:start position:0%
is a lot of the great
investments are in poorer areas

00:28:09.954 --> 00:28:12.323
because that's where you can
save people really cheaply.

00:28:12.323 --> 00:28:13.858
So one thing is, for instance,

00:28:13.858 --> 00:28:16.828
more childhood immunization,
and that's especially about

00:28:16.828 --> 00:28:19.697 align:start size:81% position:19%
so we got about
80, 90% vaccinated

00:28:19.697 --> 00:28:21.099
most places around the world.

00:28:21.099 --> 00:28:22.566
You know, measles for instance.

00:28:22.566 --> 00:28:25.436 align:start position:0%
